A||N||8||jaguiler||Coolant Temperature Sensor  - Brass Fitting?||||1||
Z||000000||jaguiler||04-29-2001||06:05 PM||||Hi,<P>Sorry to ask so many questions.  Today I was trying to install my sensor when I realized it does not actually screw in to the block.  It screws into a fitting that is in the block.... I could prob by this in the "Helpme" area of the autoshop..... but<BR>this is my only car and I noticed the hot coolant shoots out when the car is warm.....<P>Does anyone know the size of this fitting?  Or at least have an extra one I can purchase?<BR>Seems like my old sensor is frozen to the fitting.....<P>Thanks again!!! Sorry for all the questions!  I hope I can return the favor to you all one day....<BR><P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000001||jaguiler||04-30-2001||10:32 AM||||green pickles and ham||||reg||10
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||04-30-2001||01:52 PM||||Just remove the whole thing and deal with it on the outside where you can get at it better. Drain off some coolant so you don't make a mess.<P>Green eggs and sausage!||||reg||1
Z||000003||BRIAN CORNELIUS||04-30-2001||08:34 PM||||The coolant sensors fit in a 3/8" pipe threaded hole.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||10:34 PM||||are you trying to just remove the black part from the brass nut? Thats all one piece, should be no kind of fitting, the whole thing screws directly into the corner of the head.||||reg||1
Z||000005||jaguiler||05-01-2001||11:00 AM||||Well it seem like on mine the sensor screws into a fitting that is on the head.... but when I pull out my sensor the fitting comes out with it.  Does anyone else have this?<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||05-01-2001||12:53 PM||||Id just screw in the new one and throw out the old one with the fitting. Its def not supposed to be there. Maybe someone put it in to get the sensor higher and away from heat at manifold. Wont hurt I guess if youd want to keep it, any plumbing supply or hardware would have it. To me it would just be an unnecessary part to leak.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Two88GTs||05-01-2001||10:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jaguiler:<BR><B>Hi,<P>Sorry to ask so many questions.  Today I was trying to install my sensor when I realized it does not actually screw in to the block.  It screws into a fitting that is in the block.... I could prob by this in the "Helpme" area of the autoshop..... but<BR>this is my only car and I noticed the hot coolant shoots out when the car is warm.....<P>Does anyone know the size of this fitting?  Or at least have an extra one I can purchase?<BR>Seems like my old sensor is frozen to the fitting.....<P>Thanks again!!! Sorry for all the questions!  I hope I can return the favor to you all one day....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had to deal with the same problem when I replaced that sensor about a month ago; the sensor came off with the fitting like you said.  Solution: place a LARGE adjustable wrench on the fitting while having a rachet wrench with the proper-sized socket on the sensor.  Place the rachet wrench and sensor on the ground, step on it while pushing/pulling on the large adjustable wrench.  Mine came off with a "Thwack!"<BR>In hindsight, some penetrant like WD-40 or Liquid Wrench probably would have helped.<P>To clarify this info to others: this is not the fan switch sensor that is near the thermostat housing, this is the sensor that sends info back to the ECM.<P>Hope this helps you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||jaguiler||05-01-2001||10:28 PM||||Hey...thanks alot...I thought maybe I was the only one with that kind of sensor...!  Yeah I figured I could take it off like that...I thought I would just replace it though....but maybe I will try that too....thanks!<P>John<BR><P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
